timeline TD
    title History of the Eldergrove Tram Network
    section Horsecar & Early Electrification
        1880s : The Eldergrove Street Railway Company inaugurates its first horse-drawn tram service along Grand Boulevard to connect downtown with the western textile mills.
        1890s : Civic leaders commission an overhead wire electrification project to replace equine transit with faster, higher-capacity electric streetcars.
    section The Electric Golden Age
        1910s : Ridership surges to historic highs as enclosed double-truck streetcars enter regular service across twelve radiating municipal lines.
        1920s : Interurban extensions link Eldergrove with neighboring agricultural townships,<br>establishing one of the region's most comprehensive passenger transit systems.
    section Post-War Decline and Dismantlement
        1930s : Economic fallout from the Great Depression forces route consolidations, service cutbacks, and long-term deferrals of critical track maintenance.
        1950s : The city council votes to terminate rail service in favor of flexible diesel buses, paving over miles of streetcar tracks with fresh asphalt.
    section The Modern Light-Rail Renaissance
        1990s : Severe vehicular gridlock and rising air pollution prompt civic planners to launch feasibility studies exploring the return of modern electric rail.
        2020s : The state-of-the-art Eldergrove Light Rail Green Line officially opens to the public, reintroducing zero-emission rail transit to the historic urban core.
